  "query_preview": "..Showers are once again waning tonight across the\nregion outside of some lingering convection across southeast Utah\nthat should taper off just after midnight. A typical monsoonal\npattern remains in place, defined by a broad ridge overhead. The\nridge is expected to amplify overhead on Monday, with\nsoutheasterly flow aloft increasing across the region as a result.\nWhile scattered showers develop once again Monday afternoon as\nmonsoon moisture remains in place, coverage will be a bit more\nisolated with stronger steering flow aloft. As such, the flash\nflood risk is lower across the region tomorrow, though a Marginal\nRisk (level 1/4) for excessive rainfall across most of Utah and \nsouthwest Wyoming still exists, given flash flooding remains a\nthreat if a strong cell develops over sensitive terrain.\n\nThe amplifying ridge on Monday will also yield a slight bump in\ntemperatures, with highs for northern Utah valleys breaking the\ncentury mark once again. Given warm overnight lows Monday morning,\nthis will exacerbate heat impacts and raise the risk for heat\nillnesses mainly across the Wasatch Front and Great Salt Lake\nDesert.\n\nThe flash flood risk rises again Tuesday through Wednesday as the\nridge flattens in response to a trough moving into the Pacific \nNorthwest, bringing an additional surge of monsoonal moisture. \nMain concern for Tuesday exists across southern Utah, while the \nthreat shifts across northern Utah and southwest Wyoming on \nWednesday as monsoonal moisture spreads northward. While moisture\nlingers through the remainder of the week across the region, the\nbroad ridge begins to slide west through the latter half of the\nweek, introducing drier, more stable conditions which will limit\nthe potential for the daily convection chances to continue into\nthe weekend.",
